---
source: crates/kagebunshin/src/cli/mod.rs
expression: root.render_help().to_string()
---
A local secrets broker for developers and coding agents

Usage: kagebunshin [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run        Run a command with the project's secrets in its environment
  next       Print the next step toward a working setup of this checkout
  status     Show the daemon and where each key comes from
  diff       Show keys that are missing, overridden or changed
  sync       Refresh cached values from their sources
  unlock     Unlock the daemon and the local store
  lock       Lock the daemon and the local store, dropping every value held in memory
  get        Print one value (human callers only: asks for the store passphrase)
  setup      Set kagebunshin up once for this user: config, store, daemon
  uninstall  Remove kagebunshin from this machine: the service, skills and binaries
  app        Open the window: each project's keys by environment, marks and the audit log
  init       Print instructions that teach a coding agent to set up and use kagebunshin
  hook       Agent hooks: tell a session in a registered project how to run commands, and refuse writing a .env file there
  project    Create projects and add environments and sources, without editing TOML
  connect    Connect a provider: set up its credentials and check they work
  list       List what the providers can read: names, ids and keys, never values
  search     Find names or keys that contain TEXT, ignoring case (never values)
  aws        Look up AWS Secrets Manager secret names (never values)
  import     Install a mapping file someone handed you into ~/.config/kagebunshin/projects/
  onboard    Start using kagebunshin in these checkouts, with one passphrase
  export     Write a copy of a project's mapping file (no secret values) to hand to someone
  store      Manage the local encrypted store
  mark       Mark an environment, a secret or a store key sensitive
  audit      Show the audit log
  daemon     Run the daemon in the foreground or as a service, or check on it
  remote     Let other machines on your tailnet use this daemon, and say which
  help       Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --json            Print machine-readable JSON; errors go to stderr as JSON
      --socket <PATH>   Daemon socket [default: $KAGEBUNSHIN_SOCKET, else a per-user runtime dir]
      --env <ENV>       Environment to use instead of the project's default
      --project <NAME>  Project to use instead of the one for this folder; it must claim this checkout's remotes
  -h, --help            Print help
  -V, --version         Print version
